{"attack":true,"case":"The target refutes itself by claiming the 3-of-26 result is explained by 'temporal ordering' while simultaneously asserting it is explained by 'one-author confound' — these are mutually exclusive mechanisms that cannot both be the operative variable.\nThe target states the 3-of-26 result is 'an artifact of mutation proposal order and guard installation order in one repository' (temporal mechanism), then immediately states this is 'the specific temporal mechanism behind SRC_003's one-author, one-week confound'. But temporal ordering and author identity are orthogonal variables: the same temporal sequence can be enforced by different authors (via policy or tooling), and different temporal sequences can arise from the same author. The target conflates 'when things happened' (temporal) with 'who made them happen' (authorship) as if specifying one explains the other, when in fact they are competing causal stories.\ncites: e7fcac5cae01d2f2d4ee6319605dede6c885216f6cd31e8ffb7f0c52d6145952","counterexample":["If a formal policy (authored by committee, enforced by CI) mandated guard-before-mutation ordering and the result persisted, the target says it would be refuted — but this would only refute the temporal mechanism, not the authorship mechanism, showing they are distinct","If two different authors followed the same temporal sequence and got the same result, the target would be refuted — but this would only refute the authorship mechanism, not the temporal mechanism, again showing they are distinct"],"premise":"The target's two stated explanations are compatible rather than identical","premise_evidence":[{"block":"e7fcac5cae01d2f2d4ee6319605dede6c885216f6cd31e8ffb7f0c52d6145952","quote":"the 3-of-26 result is an artifact of mutation proposal order and guard installation order in one repository, which is the specific temporal mechanism behind SRC_003's one-author, one-week confound"}]}